 ("Randolph's account", ED, "Randolph described the meeting in his 2019 memoir. He recalls the price that was named, and a reaction he read as dismissive.", "Marc Randolph, That Will Never Work (2019)"),
 ("Antioco's account", ED, "John Antioco, Blockbuster's chief executive at the time, disputes that version. In 2022 he wrote that no serious conversations about buying Netflix took place.", "John Antioco, LinkedIn post, as reported by Next TV, 3 June 2022"),
 ('The number', ED, "The figure at the center of the story is fifty million dollars. It comes from Randolph's account, and it is where most retellings begin.", "Reported by Marc Randolph; not a signed or documented offer"),
 ('What really happened?', ED, "Two recollections. One number. And a question that a single meeting, however famous, cannot answer on its own.", None),
 ('Friday night', R, "To understand the decision, start with the business Blockbuster had built, and the Friday nights that built it.", None),
 ('Inside the store', R, "Its strength was physical: walls of tapes and discs, new releases in bulk, and stores close to where customers lived.", None),
 ('At the counter', R, "A customer chose a film, showed a membership card, paid at the counter, and promised to bring it back by a due date.", None),
 ('An expanding network', ED, "Repeated store by store, city by city, that simple transaction grew into a vast retail network.", None),
 ('The scale', ED, "By the end of 2004, the company described a network of roughly nine thousand one hundred stores around the world.", "Blockbuster Inc., Form 10-K for fiscal 2004 (filed March 2005)"),
 ('The rental model', R, "Every rental carried a due date, and every late return carried a fee. That small line on a receipt added up.", "Receipt values are illustrative"),
 ('Extended viewing fees', ED, "The company later reported that those extended viewing fees made up about fourteen percent of its rental revenue in 2004.", "Blockbuster Inc., Form 10-K for fiscal 2005 (filed March 2006)"),
 ('A heavy business', ED, "A system this large is powerful. It is also heavy, and heavy systems are slow to change direction when the ground moves.", None),
 ('A new approach', R, "Netflix approached the same customer problem from the other side: a disc, a paper envelope, and a mailbox.", None),
 ('In the mail', R, "There was no storefront. Orders moved through distribution centers, sorting lines and the postal system.", None),
 ('The subscription loop', ED, "Customers paid a monthly subscription, received discs, watched them, and sent them back. In this model there were no late fees.", None),
 ('Two operating models', ED, "Two businesses now served the same evening at home, for the same customers, with very different machinery working behind each of them.", None),
 ('Cost structures', ED, "One carried rent, inventory and staff across thousands of locations. The other carried postage, fulfillment, and the cost of winning subscribers.", "Conceptual comparison; not an exhaustive cost breakdown"),
 ('A growing online presence', R, "And it lived online, on early web pages and order forms, years before most viewers expected movies to arrive that way.", "Representative interface; not a recreation of any company's website"),
 ('Relative size in 2000', ED, "In 2000, the difference in scale was enormous. Blockbuster was by far the larger company, and the more established brand.", "Relative scale only; not drawn to a numerical ratio"),
 ('Was the threat obvious?', ED, "So was the threat obvious in 2000? Or does it only look obvious now?", None),
 ('August 2004', AR, "Blockbuster did respond. It moved into online rental, with a service launched in 2004 to compete directly by mail.", "Blockbuster Inc., Form 10-K for fiscal 2004"),
 ('Two systems', ED, "Now it ran two systems side by side: thousands of stores, and a growing operation built around the mailbox.", None),
 ('No late fees', ED, "In January 2005, it made a major change. It stopped charging late fees at its company-operated stores in the United States.", "Blockbuster Inc., Form 10-K for fiscal 2004"),
 ('The financial impact', AR, "The company's annual report for 2005 describes the financial impact of that decision, line by line.", "Blockbuster Inc., Form 10-K for fiscal 2005"),
 ('Costs and consequences', ED, "The company reported that the program reduced rental revenues by more than five hundred million dollars, and cost about sixty million to market and implement.", "Blockbuster Inc., Form 10-K for fiscal 2005, Risk Factors"),
 ('Total Access', AR, "In November 2006, Blockbuster introduced Total Access, its most direct answer yet to the mail-order challenge.", "Blockbuster Inc., Form 10-K for fiscal 2006"),
 ('Connecting the systems', R, "Online members could return a mailed disc at a store and leave with another film. For the first time, the two systems connected.", None),
 ('Subscriber growth', ED, "Subscribers grew quickly. So did the cost of serving them, and of the promotions that brought them in.", "Form 10-K fiscal 2006; first-quarter 2007 results release"),
